  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "The text is substantially complete, with some OCR/encoding artifacts and flattened figure/table content.",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper discusses a potential scaling trend in reasoning distillation and reports downstream benchmark scores after SFT on differently constructed corpora. It does not measure held-out pretraining cross-entropy, fit or test the stated power laws, or test compute-optimal allocation.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports benchmark accuracy for math and code models trained on corpus variants of different sizes, but these are downstream SFT outcomes, not the held-out pretraining loss or scaling relations named by the claim versions. Those results are recorded as related findings under fits_no_version. The scaling curves mentioned in Figure 1 are not extracted as claim-version results.",
    "test_reason": "The paper proposes an SFT distillation framework and reports downstream benchmark accuracy for 32B students trained on 230–1000 curated examples. It mentions a 'potential reasoning scaling law' in Figure 1 (benchmark score vs. corpus size across other published models) but fits no power law, measures no held-out loss, and varies neither parameters, tokens nor compute systematically. Its statements that results 'challenge conventional scaling laws' are rhetorical, about SFT corpus size, not pretraining scaling. No version is tested.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record's paraphrase is broadly right. The authors also stress three further points. Teacher benchmark strength and token length are not the main drivers of distillation quality. They point to corpus token entropy and the shift in latent representations instead. A small curated corpus (~0.8k) preserves out-of-domain ability while reaching SOTA on AIME, MATH-500 and LCB.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study uses SFT/distillation and evaluates downstream benchmark accuracy, rather than measuring pretraining loss or fitting scaling laws.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The reported outcomes vary across corpus construction stages and between math and code evaluation subsets.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "In SFT distillation of DS-32B, curated and diversified corpora of 230–965 examples match or exceed the full 1000-example corpus on AIME and LCB. Hard-example compression to about 230 samples causes a slight drop, and diversity expansion then recovers and exceeds the full-corpus scores.",
        "proposed_claim": "In reasoning distillation, downstream accuracy depends more on corpus selection and response diversity than on SFT corpus size, at the scale of hundreds of examples.",
